    "turn_text": "There's more to it. First of all, though, a piece of trivia. Idaho, the state of Idaho has been number one in the nation in potato production since 1951. Every year, consecutive, we're number one, and that ain't nothing. If you've ever done business in Japan, if you know the Japanese culture very well, this is not a criticism, it's an observation, and it's not bad. but the Japanese are extremely sensitive to history, tradition, loyalty, and they're very protective of their markets. They have a pretty long memory at World War II has not left that memory. And believe it or not, that's actually true in anybody who does business where Japanese knows that's the case. They are very protective of their markets. I'm old enough now, I recall some of these debates back in the 80s and 90s over micron and microchips from the Japanese semiconductor, which were such a major export there. This has been gone on a long time. And Nate, here's my personal bias. How many times have you driven to working back and not passed a whole bunch of Hondas and Toyotas on the work? Right. There is plenty of Hondas and Toyotas and Idaho and you're buying them and there's nothing wrong with that. My argument to them is, okay, we're not constraining you all. We're a big market for you. Turn around. but what's what's fair is fair there's nothing wrong with our potatoes there's no past for crying out like we ship them all over the world world-class don't give me some excuse it there's a sensitivity to a past has nothing to do with it you're protecting your markets and knock it off",
